## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of low-level synchronization, memory ordering, and backoff strategies under contention. Also checks ability to translate theory into working code and simple performance measurement.

## Key Concepts
- C11 atomics and memory_order_acquire/release
- atomic_flag spinlocks and their correctness
- exponential backoff to prevent livelock
- basic contention metrics (wall time, backoffs)

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <emmintrin.h>

static atomic_uint counter = 0;
static atomic_flag lock = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT;

void lock_spin() {
  unsigned int backoff = 1;
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&lock, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    if (backoff < 1024) backoff <<= 1;
    for (volatile unsigned int i = 0; i < backoff; ++i) _mm_pause();
  }
}

void unlock_spin() {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&lock, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, cache-line considerations, and safe reuse of barriers in multi-threaded code.

## Key Concepts

- Sense-reversing barrier pattern
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Atomic operations and memory_order semantics

## Code Example

```c
// barrier_init
void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) { b->n = n; b->count = 0; b->sense = false; }

// barrier_wait
void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, bool *local) {
    bool local_sense = !*local;
    if (atomic_fetch_add(&b->count, 1) == b->n - 1) {
        atomic_store(&b->count, 0);
        atomic_store(&b->sense, local_sense);
    } else {
        while (atomic_load(&b->sense) != local_sense) pause();
    }
    *local = local_sense;
}
```

